We all watched, over eight months, a 29-year-old with no record of work in sanctions or national security bluff his way into the world’s biggest newsrooms as a “sanctions expert.”

What @FreeBeacon found on his record: retail finance jobs at Fidelity, Schwab and JPMorgan, two lasting about three months. A firm registered to a single-family home in Arizona.

This guy joined X in March. His first post got two likes. Since then: more than 34,000 posts, about 140 a day, and 43,000 followers.

By his own count, 19 outlets have quoted him as an expert 91 times since March:

@Reuters 22 times
@washingtonpost 16 times 
Bloomberg (@business) 11 times 
@RFERL 9 times 
@politico 7 times 
@nytimes 3 times 
@FT 2 times 

Add 22 TV, radio and podcast appearances (@NewsNation 7, BBC 4, Breaking Points 2) and 8 op-eds, 6 of them in @thehill. @MeidasTouch publishes his “analysis.”

This is the man who called Iran “RIGHT” to strike ships in Hormuz. When @KatiePavlichNN reported Tehran’s call to execute me, a U.S. veteran and former Treasury official, he replied: “let’s not act like he’s an innocent actor here.”

Did any of you ask where he had ever worked on sanctions?

